WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:11.900
AMINA MANSOUR: Hello, everyone.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.500
My name is Amina Mansour,
and I'm a Solutions Architect

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:15.790
at Google Cloud.

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:18.670
Scott Surovich, who is the
Global Container Engineering

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:23.160
Lead at HSBC, will be
joining me later for a Q&amp;A.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.810
With the growing
interest and adoption

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:26.730
of hybrid and multicloud
architectures,

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:29.040
I find myself working
with a lot of customers

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.830
that are looking for guidance
on architecting their platforms

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:33.510
and choosing where
their applications

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.160
and services reside.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.070
Today we will briefly review
the difference between hybrid

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.320
and multicloud,
then I will present

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.212
some of the more common patterns
we've seen with our customers.

00:00:43.212 --> 00:00:44.920
And for the interesting
part of the talk,

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:47.128
we will have a Q&amp;A with
Scott to share his experience

00:00:47.128 --> 00:00:50.740
and insights with
hybrid and multicloud.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:53.620
So what is the difference
between hybrid and multicloud?

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:55.570
If we take hybrid
first, this is where

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:57.070
you have workloads
that are deployed

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:59.170
across multiple
environments, one

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:01.060
being a private
on-prem data center

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:04.110
and one in the public Cloud.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.690
Multicloud, on the other
hand, combines at least two

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:08.907
public Cloud environments,
but you can also

00:01:08.907 --> 00:01:10.740
have a private computing
environment as part

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:12.670
of your multicloud setup.

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:17.440
So really, multicloud is a
superset of hybrid Cloud.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:20.140
We can put these patterns
into two buckets.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:21.820
The first is
distributed deployment,

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:24.700
where the aim is to run the
application in the environment

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:25.660
that suits it best.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.970
So each application runs
in a specific environment.

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:30.610
The second is
redundant deployment,

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:32.650
where you deploy
the same application

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:35.560
in multiple environments.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.470
Let's now look at the
different patterns.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:38.970
The first pattern
we will talk about

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:42.320
is the tiered or
layered pattern.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.130
The idea of it is to
focus first on deploying

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:47.620
front-end applications
to the public Cloud.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:50.230
In this pattern, you keep your
back-end applications and data

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:53.500
in the private
computing environment.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.330
Some advantages are
that this allows

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:57.565
you to start with the
less complex migrations.

00:01:57.565 --> 00:01:59.440
You also benefit from
what Google Cloud knows

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:03.240
what to do well, so things like
load balancing and autoscaling.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.130
And you keep your data
and back-end on-prem

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.620
for regulatory reasons.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:09.780
Because you are communicating
across environments,

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.210
choose the Google Cloud Regions
and Interconnect location

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.220
closest to your data center.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:16.650
Make sure to secure the
communication with VPN tunnels,

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:19.650
TLS, or both, and
establish a common identity

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:23.650
between environments
to authenticate across.

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:26.836
The second pattern is
the partition pattern.

00:02:26.836 --> 00:02:29.433
It combines multiple
public cloud environments

00:02:29.433 --> 00:02:31.100
that gives you the
flexibility to deploy

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:34.320
an application in the best
computing environment for it.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.600
So application A is on Cloud 1
and application B is on Cloud 2

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:43.188
based on its dependencies
and Cloud services it uses.

00:02:43.188 --> 00:02:45.230
Because of your presence
in multiple public Cloud

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:47.450
environments, you're
lowering your risk

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:50.780
and you have the flexibility to
change plans or partnerships.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:52.460
You can also choose
which provider

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:57.180
to run an application or service
on depending on its needs.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.810
Each additional environment
comes with additional overhead,

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:02.730
so weigh the advantages
against the overhead.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:05.050
You should also minimize
dependencies across Clouds

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:07.080
so it doesn't
affect performance.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.750
And focus less on portability
of your applications

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:12.000
and more on the portability
of your workflows

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.000
and having a unified
platform across providers.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.280
Our third pattern is edge.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.140
Running workloads in
the Cloud requires

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:25.710
that clients have fast and
reliable internet connectivity.

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:28.020
There are scenarios when you
cannot rely on continuous

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:31.050
connectivity, such as stores or
supermarkets that might be only

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:33.690
connected occasionally or use
links that are not reliable

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.340
enough for business
critical transactions.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:39.310
With this pattern, you run time-
and business-critical workloads

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:41.020
locally at the
edge of the network

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:44.530
while using the Cloud for
all other kinds of workloads.

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:46.450
In an edge setup,
the internet link

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:48.160
is a non-critical
component that is

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.620
used for management purposes
and to synchronize your upload

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:53.250
data.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:55.850
This helps you ensure low
latency and self-sufficiency

00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:59.330
in cases when you cannot do that
due to network restrictions.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:01.700
You also reuse existing
investments and equipment

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:03.115
at those edge locations.

00:04:03.115 --> 00:04:05.240
And remember that increased
traffic to Google Cloud

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.198
is free, so that makes
it easier to communicate

00:04:07.198 --> 00:04:11.300
back to the Cloud with
status or data syncs.

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:13.225
Two things to keep in
mind with this pattern--

00:04:13.225 --> 00:04:15.350
you don't want to increase
the burden of management

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:18.232
of these edge locations
linearly with how many they are,

00:04:18.232 --> 00:04:20.690
so you should have a centralized
control plane in the Cloud

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:22.370
to manage them all.

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:24.770
Also, the less dependencies
on the Cloud environment,

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:29.200
the more reliable and
faster your edge setup is.

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:32.360
Our fourth pattern is analytics.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:34.520
In this pattern,
transactional workloads

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.980
stay on-prem and
analytics workloads

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:39.845
are in the public Cloud to
leverage best of read services.

00:04:39.845 --> 00:04:42.470
Of course, the first thing that
comes to mind with this pattern

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:44.990
is BigQuery, which is a very
common reason why our customers

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:47.980
choose this pattern.

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:50.920
Analytics workloads often need
to process huge amounts of data

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:52.960
and can be bursty, so
they lend themselves

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:54.730
well to the public
Cloud and you don't

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:57.630
need to overprovision
on-prem anymore.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.190
Google Cloud also
provides a rich set

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:01.800
of services to manage
data throughout its entire

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.930
lifecycle, ranging from
initial acquisition,

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:07.900
to processing and analyzing,
to final visualization.

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:10.372
Also you get to take advantage
of Cloud capabilities--

00:05:10.372 --> 00:05:12.580
again, like I mentioned,
increased traffic and moving

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:15.038
data from the private computing
environment to Google Cloud

00:05:15.038 --> 00:05:15.802
is free of charge.

00:05:15.802 --> 00:05:18.010
And when you have existing
Hadoop or Spark workloads,

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:19.840
consider migrating
jobs to data PROC

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:22.840
and migrating existing
HDFS data to Cloud Storage.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.000
Use queues to hand over data.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.880
So things like Pub/Sub or maybe
even Cloud Storage buckets

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:32.380
to hand over the
data to Google Cloud.

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:34.380
Choose the transfer
approach that is best suited

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:36.630
for your data set size
and available bandwidth

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:39.060
for the initial data
transfer, and you

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:40.950
should use consistent
tooling and processes

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:42.750
across environments
to help increase

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:44.490
your operational efficiency.

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:48.680
This one is very common for
a majority of the patterns.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:50.720
Last but not least is
the bursting pattern,

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:52.670
and it's the only
pattern discussed today

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:56.350
that fits in the redundant
deployment pattern bucket.

00:05:56.350 --> 00:05:58.002
The idea of the Cloud
bursting pattern

00:05:58.002 --> 00:05:59.710
is to use a private
computing environment

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:02.080
for the baseline load
and burst to the Cloud

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:04.890
temporarily when you
need that extra capacity.

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:07.390
While you can accommodate bursty
workloads in a classic data

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:09.310
center-based
computing environment,

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:11.050
by overprovisioning
your resources,

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:13.630
this approach is not
really cost effective.

00:06:13.630 --> 00:06:16.530
So this pattern
allows you to apply it

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:18.510
to interactive and
batch workloads.

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:20.640
But when you're dealing
with interactive workloads,

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:22.515
you must determine how
to distribute requests

00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:25.268
across environments.

00:06:25.268 --> 00:06:28.100
These three advantages
are all related.

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:29.840
It allows you to
reuse, or in this case,

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:32.330
use your investments,
and there is no longer

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:35.030
a need to overprovision your
compute resources on-prem.

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:37.250
So that enables increased
utilization and cost

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:41.590
effectiveness in the private
computing environment.

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:43.317
As for best
practices, again, you

00:06:43.317 --> 00:06:45.400
should choose the regions
that are close together.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:49.060
That applies to the Cloud region
and the Interconnect location.

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:52.000
You should make Cloud resources
private for batch workloads.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.980
Those are not public-facing,
and so in this case,

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:57.580
you should secure and
have them be private.

00:06:57.580 --> 00:06:59.320
And you should secure
the communication

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:02.110
because the data that is
exchanged between environments

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:03.730
might be sensitive.

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:05.710
And finally, establish
common identity

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:07.420
so that you can
securely authenticate

00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:09.868
across environment boundaries.

00:07:09.868 --> 00:07:11.660
Now it's time for the
more interesting part

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:12.770
of our session.

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:14.690
Scott is the Global
Container Engineering

00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:18.200
Lead at HSBC and the Global
Anthos Product Owner.

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:19.730
He's also a Google
Cloud Certified

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:22.850
Fellow and Co-Chair of the
CNCF Financial Services Working

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:23.930
Group.

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:25.897
Great to have you with
us here today, Scott.

00:07:25.897 --> 00:07:26.980
SCOTT SUROVICH: Hi, Amina.

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:28.227
Thanks for having me.

00:07:28.227 --> 00:07:30.310
AMINA MANSOUR: Before we
start with our questions,

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:34.580
why don't you tell us a little
bit about the scale of HSBC?

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:35.702
SCOTT SUROVICH: Sure.

00:07:35.702 --> 00:07:37.160
So before we get
into the patterns,

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:39.500
I'll explain about who HSBC is.

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:42.050
We are one of the world's
largest global banks,

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:45.380
and the sheer scale of what
we deal with on a daily basis

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:47.370
is presented in this slide.

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:50.000
As you can see, we're
in 66 countries.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.960
We process about $1.5
trillion a day in payments.

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:56.960
We have 110,000 servers,
roughly, globally

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:59.090
right now-- so that's
an out of date portion.

00:07:59.090 --> 00:08:02.240
We have data centers in
21 different countries--

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:04.820
that does not count where we
have large server rooms, as

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:05.780
well.

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:09.950
We have a large amount of
employees, around 235,000.

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:12.350
We've got 39 million
customers globally.

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:16.370
We process about revenue
of $53.8 billion a year,

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:18.710
and we have offices all over
the world-- roughly about

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:20.400
3,900 offices.

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:21.950
So it's a large organization.

00:08:21.950 --> 00:08:25.780
And even in the IT section, we
have 40,000 IT professionals

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:28.272
working for us.

00:08:28.272 --> 00:08:30.230
AMINA MANSOUR: Thank you
for that intro, Scott.

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:32.510
Let me start by asking
you why you would consider

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:36.395
a hybrid or multicloud solution
in a highly-regulated industry?

00:08:36.395 --> 00:08:38.020
SCOTT SUROVICH:
Definitely, great idea.

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:39.437
The first thing
I'll talk about is

00:08:39.437 --> 00:08:40.720
going to be the hybrid Cloud.

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:43.000
So in the hybrid Cloud,
as we have on the slide,

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:44.800
we have data centers globally.

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:47.720
And again, we have server
rooms globally, as well.

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:51.280
So we have a large investment
in data centers already.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:53.385
So we want to utilize
that where we can.

00:08:53.385 --> 00:08:54.760
But of course,
going to the Cloud

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:57.770
makes sense in
multiple scenarios.

00:08:57.770 --> 00:09:01.300
So to use these data centers,
we have a hybrid Cloud solution.

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:03.230
It helps us with a backup plan.

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:05.500
So if we go to a Cloud
with an application,

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:07.600
we have to have a backup
plan for the regulators.

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:09.280
So we have to show
that if something

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:14.110
were to go wrong in the
links, the relationship, just

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:16.570
with the vendor in general,
where do we go from there?

00:09:16.570 --> 00:09:19.240
And on-prem is a
viable solution.

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:22.060
It also could go to
another CSV, but since we

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:25.990
have those data centers, we
can leverage that internally.

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:29.380
Finally, it takes time to get
approval to go to the Cloud

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:30.610
for an application.

00:09:30.610 --> 00:09:33.130
It can take three to six months.

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:35.980
Now, in that time, you
don't want to sit idle.

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:38.050
You might want to
refactor your app.

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:40.100
You might want to test
out some new services.

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:43.240
So with the introduction
of containers--

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:45.850
Kubernetes and
Anthos-- for on-prem,

00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:48.670
if a workload fits into
that, my developers

00:09:48.670 --> 00:09:51.100
can actually start
developing that today

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:52.600
and they can move
that to the Cloud

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:55.420
once they have the
approval to do so.

00:09:55.420 --> 00:09:59.290
In the multicloud side, we
select the best-of-breed

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:02.500
for features for each CSP.

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:05.590
As you said, BigQuery is a
big one for the Google side,

00:10:05.590 --> 00:10:07.690
and it's just one of
many different features

00:10:07.690 --> 00:10:09.620
on the Google side.

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:11.710
There's also regional
availability.

00:10:11.710 --> 00:10:13.480
Certain times I
have an application

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:15.880
that has to go into
multiple regions.

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:19.750
But somebody like Google may
not have a presence in China,

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:21.970
or you may not have
a point of presence

00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:24.140
somewhere close to a
certain other region.

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:27.820
So we have to go multicloud
where it makes sense

00:10:27.820 --> 00:10:29.527
to go multicloud.

00:10:29.527 --> 00:10:31.610
AMINA MANSOUR: Those are
all great reasons, Scott.

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:32.985
Of course, we
recognize there are

00:10:32.985 --> 00:10:35.360
many other reasons for other
industries and verticals.

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:38.000
But of those patterns that I
described, which patterns are

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:40.005
you most likely to implement?

00:10:40.005 --> 00:10:40.880
SCOTT SUROVICH: Sure.

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:41.780
All the models are great.

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:42.905
All the patterns are great.

00:10:42.905 --> 00:10:45.260
So the first one that
I like to talk about

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:48.980
is going to be the tiered
model, or the tiered pattern.

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:53.210
So in my previous days, we
would have our websites on-site.

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:55.830
And we would be under attack--
get denial of service attacks.

00:10:55.830 --> 00:10:57.650
We'd have to engage
multiple teams--

00:10:57.650 --> 00:11:00.530
operating systems, networking,
obviously our service

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:04.340
providers, and we would
spend hours and hours trying

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:06.890
to remediate that situation.

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:08.930
We want to restore the
services to our customers

00:11:08.930 --> 00:11:10.680
and our employees.

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:13.730
So rather than have us deal
with that-- the whole time we're

00:11:13.730 --> 00:11:16.310
fighting that fire,
we are not innovating

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:18.510
and we're not going
forward for the business.

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:20.720
So let's let
somebody like Google,

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:22.730
who was born in the Cloud,
raised in the Cloud,

00:11:22.730 --> 00:11:25.880
knows the Cloud, and
can protect the Cloud--

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:29.540
we will put our
presentation layer in GCP

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:31.790
and products like the
Web Application Firewall,

00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:35.480
available on Cloud
Armor, would help

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:38.540
us to remediate that
attack a lot faster

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:41.180
and not drain our resources.

00:11:41.180 --> 00:11:43.280
So put the front end
in the CSP, but keeping

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:47.930
your data safe, and even
better, regulatory compliance

00:11:47.930 --> 00:11:50.590
to have your data on-prem.

00:11:50.590 --> 00:11:53.397
The other model would
be the partition model.

00:11:53.397 --> 00:11:55.730
Now, we've already mentioned
that HSBC has multiple data

00:11:55.730 --> 00:11:58.160
centers and multiple
server rooms

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:01.405
globally, so we have a heavy
investment in machines.

00:12:01.405 --> 00:12:03.530
AMINA MANSOUR: But what
about the bursting pattern?

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:06.490
From your experience, what
are some use cases for that?

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:08.240
SCOTT SUROVICH: Yeah,
the bursting pattern

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:09.770
is a great use case for Cloud.

00:12:09.770 --> 00:12:13.400
However, it's also one of the
more difficult ones to utilize.

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:15.200
And I say that
because the challenge

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:19.040
is the regulatory issues that
we deal with in this industry.

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:21.880
So because of that, I can't
just select any workload

00:12:21.880 --> 00:12:24.380
and say, OK, I'm going to burst
when I need to in the Cloud.

00:12:24.380 --> 00:12:27.270
It has to be an application
that is approved,

00:12:27.270 --> 00:12:28.680
the data has to be approved.

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:30.210
So we have to be careful.

00:12:30.210 --> 00:12:34.190
So with the right choice,
it's the perfect scenario

00:12:34.190 --> 00:12:36.830
for the challenges
that it addresses.

00:12:36.830 --> 00:12:40.200
And 2020 was one of
those challenges.

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:42.500
So, of course, during
2020, a lot of people

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:43.820
started working from home.

00:12:43.820 --> 00:12:46.190
Basically everybody
started working from home.

00:12:46.190 --> 00:12:49.220
If you had to expand your
data center for certain use

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:51.560
cases because of this
different working pattern,

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:53.900
you might have to
expand out and scale out

00:12:53.900 --> 00:12:55.610
your server infrastructure.

00:12:55.610 --> 00:12:58.040
That was a big
challenge in 2020.

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:00.080
Trying to order
equipment was delayed

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:04.220
for months due to
manufacturing, due to shipping,

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:06.630
or due to silicone shortages.

00:13:06.630 --> 00:13:09.030
So we can't wait
months for that.

00:13:09.030 --> 00:13:12.312
So to address this, first
thing is the perfect scenario.

00:13:12.312 --> 00:13:14.270
It's a little bit longer
than normal temporary,

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:15.812
kind of like what
you were mentioning

00:13:15.812 --> 00:13:17.930
during your introduction,
but at some point

00:13:17.930 --> 00:13:21.285
we do hope that all of
this is going to die down,

00:13:21.285 --> 00:13:22.910
everything's going
to be good, and then

00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:24.890
we all get back to
the office-- we don't

00:13:24.890 --> 00:13:26.760
need all that extra capacity.

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:30.620
So by scaling up and
bursting into the Cloud,

00:13:30.620 --> 00:13:33.650
we do it faster,
we do it cheaper,

00:13:33.650 --> 00:13:36.020
and we don't have any of
that hardware left over

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:41.147
after we're done with all
of the nightmare of 2020.

00:13:41.147 --> 00:13:41.980
AMINA MANSOUR: 100%.

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:44.050
You hit the nail on the
head with this example.

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:45.820
But let's pivot to
something happier.

00:13:45.820 --> 00:13:48.787
What's a fun use case
for the edge pattern?

00:13:48.787 --> 00:13:50.370
SCOTT SUROVICH: Yeah,
the edge pattern

00:13:50.370 --> 00:13:52.110
is one I've always liked.

00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:55.170
So in the banking industry,
a lot of processes,

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:57.510
a lot of hardware,
started to look at that.

00:13:57.510 --> 00:14:00.280
So ATMs-- we're not
quite there yet,

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:02.730
but that's a great
use case for an edge.

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:06.420
From an HSBC view, though, the
edge case I like to bring up

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:08.190
is something called Pepper.

00:14:08.190 --> 00:14:11.100
Pepper is actually a
robot, originally developed

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:14.970
by SoftBank, that we
have in 22 branches.

00:14:14.970 --> 00:14:17.550
And what Pepper does is
it will greet the customer

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:19.090
as they come in.

00:14:19.090 --> 00:14:21.130
So banking isn't exciting.

00:14:21.130 --> 00:14:22.470
We all kind of know that.

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:24.425
Pepper makes it exciting.

00:14:24.425 --> 00:14:25.800
So when you walk
into this branch

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:28.350
and you see this robot with
a really cute face looking

00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:32.190
at you with an LCD and touch
screen, you can walk up to her

00:14:32.190 --> 00:14:36.060
and you can choose what you
want to talk about to CSR

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:40.380
or what products you
might be interested in.

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:42.390
Not only is it fun
and interactive,

00:14:42.390 --> 00:14:45.090
but it cuts the wait time
down for the customer.

00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:47.223
This all goes into a system
that the CSR will see

00:14:47.223 --> 00:14:49.140
and then they can walk
out to get you and know

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:51.990
exactly what you need
before four other people ask

00:14:51.990 --> 00:14:53.213
you the same questions.

00:14:53.213 --> 00:14:55.380
You go right to the person,
they know what you need,

00:14:55.380 --> 00:14:58.830
and they can process your
request really quick.

00:14:58.830 --> 00:15:00.450
Now aside from the cool factor--

00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:02.590
it does bring people
into the branch.

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:04.080
So people want to see Pepper--

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:05.730
taking pictures, posting them.

00:15:05.730 --> 00:15:08.070
We get our brand out there.

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:10.740
But it can go further than
just this basic stuff.

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:15.390
So internally, Pepper is limited
to about four core M processor

00:15:15.390 --> 00:15:16.350
with 4 gigs of RAM.

00:15:16.350 --> 00:15:18.330
It can do a lot of processing.

00:15:18.330 --> 00:15:21.060
It can talk to systems
locally so we can figure out

00:15:21.060 --> 00:15:22.620
what we need for you.

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:24.720
But to make it more
fun and to expand it,

00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:27.270
we can connect that
into the Cloud.

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:29.140
So of course, using
something like Google,

00:15:29.140 --> 00:15:34.980
we can use translation
services, text-to-speech, AI

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:35.857
interactions.

00:15:35.857 --> 00:15:37.440
And one of my favorite
ones that we've

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:41.770
been debating, with consent,
would be facial recognition.

00:15:41.770 --> 00:15:44.640
So Pepper can greet you when
you walk into the branch

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:46.980
and it can say,
"Welcome back, Amina.

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:48.900
Would you like to
look at this service?"

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:51.330
Or just having a
conversation with you

00:15:51.330 --> 00:15:53.890
using the AI interaction.

00:15:53.890 --> 00:15:55.140
AMINA MANSOUR: That's amazing.

00:15:55.140 --> 00:15:57.720
I personally hope to
meet Pepper someday.

00:15:57.720 --> 00:16:00.480
I know that HSBC leverages
BigQuery for some analytics

00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:02.220
workloads, so tell
us a little bit more

00:16:02.220 --> 00:16:04.195
about the analytics
pattern there.

00:16:04.195 --> 00:16:05.070
SCOTT SUROVICH: Yeah.

00:16:05.070 --> 00:16:06.942
We're a bank, we
get a lot of data.

00:16:06.942 --> 00:16:09.150
We have a lot of things that
we have to sift through,

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:11.970
look at, do analysis on.

00:16:11.970 --> 00:16:15.030
So BigQuery is very popular
among our developers,

00:16:15.030 --> 00:16:17.500
and that's at a global scale.

00:16:17.500 --> 00:16:22.380
So with BigQuery, as you said
during the introductions,

00:16:22.380 --> 00:16:25.800
it can process your data,
handle millions of queries

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:29.250
per second, and without
us having to expand

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:31.125
and install hardware for this.

00:16:31.125 --> 00:16:33.000
Now, in the earlier
days, we used to do this.

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:36.000
We would run things that
would take two weeks.

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:39.330
It would run at a bunch of
kit that we also had DR for,

00:16:39.330 --> 00:16:41.730
because if we didn't
have a problem of fraud,

00:16:41.730 --> 00:16:44.220
we'd have to put it to
DR. It's a lot of money

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:47.980
that when you're not processing
anything, it just sits there.

00:16:47.980 --> 00:16:52.020
So aside from just being
slower and costing more,

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:54.067
we wanted to go to
the Cloud for that.

00:16:54.067 --> 00:16:55.650
So one of our
applications that we did

00:16:55.650 --> 00:16:59.460
happen to move used to take
about 10 days to process

00:16:59.460 --> 00:17:00.750
the information.

00:17:00.750 --> 00:17:03.870
It sat on a lot of kit, and
for the rest of that month

00:17:03.870 --> 00:17:05.910
it just sat.

00:17:05.910 --> 00:17:09.930
So it takes up power,
it takes up cooling,

00:17:09.930 --> 00:17:11.490
it just takes up
general maintenance.

00:17:11.490 --> 00:17:15.329
We have to pay for a lot
of stuff that isn't in use.

00:17:15.329 --> 00:17:18.960
By moving it over to
BigQuery, we only utilize

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:21.510
or we only get charged
when we're utilizing it.

00:17:21.510 --> 00:17:24.329
It also cut our processing time
on that operation from those

00:17:24.329 --> 00:17:28.140
10 days down to less than a
day, and on certain operations

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:29.950
down to a couple of hours.

00:17:29.950 --> 00:17:31.410
So we get the
information analyzed

00:17:31.410 --> 00:17:33.780
much quicker and cheaper.

00:17:33.780 --> 00:17:35.880
And there really is
nothing better, and faster,

00:17:35.880 --> 00:17:38.075
and cheaper, and it's done well.

00:17:38.075 --> 00:17:40.200
AMINA MANSOUR: That's a
great use case for BigQuery

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:41.880
and the analytics pattern.

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:44.205
Thank you so much for
joining us today, Scott.

00:17:44.205 --> 00:17:46.080
It is great to learn
about all the innovation

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:48.773
that Google Cloud products
enable for our customers.

00:17:48.773 --> 00:17:49.690
SCOTT SUROVICH: Great.

00:17:49.690 --> 00:17:50.273
Thanks, Amina.

00:17:50.273 --> 00:17:51.460
It's my pleasure to be here.

00:17:51.460 --> 00:17:53.710
AMINA MANSOUR: If you'd like
to continue learning more

00:17:53.710 --> 00:17:56.710
about the different
multicloud and hybrid patterns

00:17:56.710 --> 00:17:59.560
and how to implement those
using Google Cloud products,

00:17:59.560 --> 00:18:01.360
please check out
these sessions here

00:18:01.360 --> 00:18:04.630
that will talk about Anthos,
for example, as the control

00:18:04.630 --> 00:18:07.660
plane supporting that
multicloud or hybrid patterns,

00:18:07.660 --> 00:18:09.970
as well as different
examples with Cloud Run

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:12.370
running in hybrid or
multicloud environments,

00:18:12.370 --> 00:18:15.430
all the way to
extending your Anthos

00:18:15.430 --> 00:18:20.600
or multicloud environment to VM
workloads on-premises, as well.

00:18:20.600 --> 00:18:22.600
Thank you so much for
joining the session today,

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:24.990
and hopefully it was useful.

